al dilemmas. Critics argue that without a unifying moral theory, principlis=
m may lack the depth required for resolving complex ethical issues. However=
, its strength lies in its practical applicability and its ability to facil=
itate consensus among diverse ethical perspectives.</p><h2>3) Principle-by-=
